#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:18
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"'git' [-v | --version] [-h | --help] [-C <path>] [-c <name>=<value>]\n"
"    [--exec-path[=<path>]] [--html-path] [--man-path] [--info-path]\n"
"    [-p | --paginate | -P | --no-pager] [--no-replace-objects] "
"[--no-lazy-fetch]\n"
"    [--no-optional-locks] [--no-advice] [--bare] [--git-dir=<path>]\n"
"    [--work-tree=<path>] [--namespace=<name>] "
"[--config-env=<name>=<envvar>]\n"
"    <command> [<args>]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:24
msgid ""
"Git is a fast, scalable, distributed revision control system with an "
"unusually rich command set that provides both high-level operations and full "
"access to internals."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:29
msgid ""
"See linkgit:gittutorial[7] to get started, then see linkgit:giteveryday[7] "
"for a useful minimum set of commands.  The link:user-manual.html[Git User's "
"Manual] has a more in-depth introduction."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:34
msgid ""
"After you mastered the basic concepts, you can come back to this page to "
"learn what commands Git offers.  You can learn more about individual Git "
"commands with \"git help command\".  linkgit:gitcli[7] manual page gives you "
"an overview of the command-line command syntax."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:38
msgid ""
"A formatted and hyperlinked copy of the latest Git documentation can be "
"viewed at https://git.github.io/htmldocs/git.html or "
"https://git-scm.com/docs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:41
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:45
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-v::`\n"
"`--version::`\n"
"\tPrints the Git suite version that the 'git' program came from.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:49
msgid ""
"This option is internally converted to `git version ...` and accepts the "
"same options as the linkgit:git-version[1] command. If `--help` is also "
"given, it takes precedence over `--version`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:56
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-h::`\n"
"`--help::`\n"
"\tPrints the synopsis and a list of the most commonly used\n"
"\tcommands. If the option `--all` or `-a` is given then all\n"
"\tavailable commands are printed. If a Git command is named this\n"
"\toption will bring up the manual page for that command.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:61
msgid ""
"Other options are available to control how the manual page is displayed. See "
"linkgit:git-help[1] for more information, because `git --help ...` is "
"converted internally into `git help ...`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:68
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-C`\n"
"\tRun as if git was started in '<path>' instead of the current working\n"
"\tdirectory.  When multiple `-C` options are given, each subsequent\n"
"\tnon-absolute `-C <path>` is interpreted relative to the preceding `-C\n"
"\t<path>`.  If '<path>' is present but empty, e.g. `-C \"\"`, then the\n"
"\tcurrent working directory is left unchanged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:73
msgid ""
"This option affects options that expect path name like `--git-dir` and "
"`--work-tree` in that their interpretations of the path names would be made "
"relative to the working directory caused by the `-C` option. For example the "
"following invocations are equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    git --git-dir=a.git --work-tree=b -C c status\n"
"    git --git-dir=c/a.git --work-tree=c/b status\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-c`\n"
"\tPass a configuration parameter to the command. The value\n"
"\tgiven will override values from configuration files.\n"
"\tThe <name> is expected in the same format as listed by\n"
"\t'git config' (subkeys separated by dots).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:88
msgid ""
"Note that omitting the `=` in `git -c foo.bar ...` is allowed and sets "
"`foo.bar` to the boolean true value (just like `[foo]bar` would in a config "
"file). Including the equals but with an empty value (like `git -c foo.bar= "
"...`) sets `foo.bar` to the empty string which `git config --type=bool` will "
"convert to `false`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--config-env=<name>=<envvar>::`\n"
"\tLike `-c <name>=<value>`, give configuration variable\n"
"\t'<name>' a value, where <envvar> is the name of an\n"
"\tenvironment variable from which to retrieve the value. Unlike\n"
"\t`-c` there is no shortcut for directly setting the value to an\n"
"\tempty string, instead the environment variable itself must be\n"
"\tset to the empty string.  It is an error if the `<envvar>` does not "
"exist\n"
"\tin the environment. `<envvar>` may not contain an equals sign\n"
"\tto avoid ambiguity with `<name>` containing one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:105
msgid ""
"This is useful for cases where you want to pass transitory configuration "
"options to git, but are doing so on operating systems where other processes "
"might be able to read your command line (e.g. `/proc/self/cmdline`), but not "
"your environment (e.g. `/proc/self/environ`). That behavior is the default "
"on Linux, but may not be on your system."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:110
msgid ""
"Note that this might add security for variables such as `http.extraHeader` "
"where the sensitive information is part of the value, but not "
"e.g. `url.<base>.insteadOf` where the sensitive information can be part of "
"the key."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:116
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--exec-path[=<path>]::`\n"
"\tPath to wherever your core Git programs are installed.\n"
"\tThis can also be controlled by setting the GIT_EXEC_PATH\n"
"\tenvironment variable. If no path is given, 'git' will print\n"
"\tthe current setting and then exit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:120
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--html-path::`\n"
"\tPrint the path, without trailing slash, where Git's HTML\n"
"\tdocumentation is installed and exit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--man-path::`\n"
"\tPrint the manpath (see `man(1)`) for the man pages for\n"
"\tthis version of Git and exit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--info-path::`\n"
"\tPrint the path where the Info files documenting this\n"
"\tversion of Git are installed and exit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:135
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-p::`\n"
"`--paginate::`\n"
"\tPipe all output into 'less' (or if set, $PAGER) if standard\n"
"\toutput is a terminal.  This overrides the `pager.<cmd>`\n"
"\tconfiguration options (see the \"Configuration Mechanism\" section\n"
"\tbelow).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:139
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`-P::`\n"
"`--no-pager::`\n"
"\tDo not pipe Git output into a pager.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:144
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--git-dir=<path>::`\n"
"\tSet the path to the repository (\".git\" directory). This can also be\n"
"\tcontrolled by setting the `GIT_DIR` environment variable. It can be\n"
"\tan absolute path or relative path to current working directory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:155
msgid ""
"Specifying the location of the \".git\" directory using this option (or "
"`GIT_DIR` environment variable) turns off the repository discovery that "
"tries to find a directory with \".git\" subdirectory (which is how the "
"repository and the top-level of the working tree are discovered), and tells "
"Git that you are at the top level of the working tree.  If you are not at "
"the top-level directory of the working tree, you should tell Git where the "
"top-level of the working tree is, with the `--work-tree=<path>` option (or "
"`GIT_WORK_TREE` environment variable)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:158
msgid ""
"If you just want to run git as if it was started in `<path>` then use `git "
"-C <path>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:166
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--work-tree=<path>::`\n"
"\tSet the path to the working tree. It can be an absolute path\n"
"\tor a path relative to the current working directory.\n"
"\tThis can also be controlled by setting the GIT_WORK_TREE\n"
"\tenvironment variable and the core.worktree configuration\n"
"\tvariable (see core.worktree in linkgit:git-config[1] for a\n"
"\tmore detailed discussion).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:171
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--namespace=<path>::`\n"
"\tSet the Git namespace.  See linkgit:gitnamespaces[7] for more\n"
"\tdetails.  Equivalent to setting the `GIT_NAMESPACE` environment\n"
"\tvariable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:176
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--bare::`\n"
"\tTreat the repository as a bare repository.  If GIT_DIR\n"
"\tenvironment is not set, it is set to the current working\n"
"\tdirectory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:182
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--no-replace-objects::`\n"
"\tDo not use replacement refs to replace Git objects.\n"
"\tThis is equivalent to exporting the `GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS`\n"
"\tenvironment variable with any value.\n"
"\tSee linkgit:git-replace[1] for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--no-lazy-fetch::`\n"
"\tDo not fetch missing objects from the promisor remote on\n"
"\tdemand.  Useful together with `git cat-file -e <object>` to\n"
"\tsee if the object is locally available.\n"
"\tThis is equivalent to setting the `GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH`\n"
"\tenvironment variable to `1`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:193
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--no-optional-locks::`\n"
"\tDo not perform optional operations that require locks. This is\n"
"\tequivalent to setting the `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS` to `0`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:196
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--no-advice::`\n"
"\tDisable all advice hints from being printed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:201
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--literal-pathspecs::`\n"
"\tTreat pathspecs literally (i.e. no globbing, no pathspec magic).\n"
"\tThis is equivalent to setting the `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS` environment\n"
"\tvariable to `1`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--glob-pathspecs::`\n"
"\tAdd \"glob\" magic to all pathspec. This is equivalent to setting\n"
"\tthe `GIT_GLOB_PATHSPECS` environment variable to `1`. Disabling\n"
"\tglobbing on individual pathspecs can be done using pathspec\n"
"\tmagic \":(literal)\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:213
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--noglob-pathspecs::`\n"
"\tAdd \"literal\" magic to all pathspec. This is equivalent to setting\n"
"\tthe `GIT_NOGLOB_PATHSPECS` environment variable to `1`. Enabling\n"
"\tglobbing on individual pathspecs can be done using pathspec\n"
"\tmagic \":(glob)\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:217
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--icase-pathspecs::`\n"
"\tAdd \"icase\" magic to all pathspec. This is equivalent to setting\n"
"\tthe `GIT_ICASE_PATHSPECS` environment variable to `1`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:227
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--list-cmds=<group>[,<group>...]::`\n"
"\tList commands by group. This is an internal/experimental\n"
"\toption and may change or be removed in the future. Supported\n"
"\tgroups are: builtins, parseopt (builtin commands that use\n"
"\tparse-options), main (all commands in libexec directory),\n"
"\tothers (all other commands in `$PATH` that have git- prefix),\n"
"\tlist-<category> (see categories in command-list.txt),\n"
"\tnohelpers (exclude helper commands), alias and config\n"
"\t(retrieve command list from config variable completion.commands)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git.txt:232
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--attr-source=<tree-ish>::`\n"
"\tRead gitattributes from <tree-ish> instead of the worktree. See\n"
"\tlinkgit:gitattributes[5]. This is equivalent to setting the\n"
"\t`GIT_ATTR_SOURCE` environment variable.\n"
msgstr ""

